package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

internal object StoryLibraryBatch68 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries(
            id = "the_atlas_of_quiet_places",
            title = "The Atlas of Quiet Places",
            tagline = "A student maps the overlooked corners that help a neighbourhood feel like home.",
            parts = listOf(
                StoryPart(1, "The Blank Page", listOf("When Mara's geography teacher assigned a neighbourhood map, everyone chose busy roads, shops, and landmarks. Mara opened her notebook and found that the most important places in her life were missing from every official map: the shaded bench behind the clinic, the little reading room above the market, and the steps where neighbours exchanged seedlings.", "She began drawing them in the margins. Her friend Dami noticed and asked why she was mapping places that had no names. Mara answered that perhaps they had names, but nobody had thought to write them down.", "That afternoon, an elderly woman at the bus stop saw the notebook and pointed to a narrow lane. 'You have forgotten the place where people leave what they can spare,' she said. Mara added a small square and labelled it the Sharing Shelf.")),
                StoryPart(2, "The Bench Under the Neem", listOf("The bench behind the clinic had been repaired so many times that none of its boards matched. A handwritten sign asked visitors to leave the seat free for anyone waiting for news or needing a quiet moment.", "Mara interviewed the caretaker, who explained that the bench had been built by local carpenters after the clinic expanded. People had donated wood, paint, and time. No single person owned it, which was why the council's map had ignored it.", "Dami photographed the bench while Mara recorded its story. They agreed that every place on the atlas needed more than a dot. It needed the voices of the people who cared for it.")),
                StoryPart(3, "The Room Above the Market", listOf("The reading room was reached by a staircase beside a fruit stall. Inside, shelves leaned at different angles and a ceiling fan turned slowly above a table covered with donated books.", "Its volunteer librarian, Mrs Eze, told them that the room had started with twelve books in a wooden crate. Traders had offered the unused upstairs space, and schoolchildren had organised the first catalogue.", "Mara found a notebook in which readers had written recommendations instead of names. One message said, 'This room made the city feel smaller.' She copied the sentence carefully and asked permission to include it.")),
                StoryPart(4, "A Map with Too Many Pins", listOf("After a week, Mara and Dami had marked twenty-seven places. Their classmates suggested adding every popular shop and sports ground, but the map was becoming crowded and losing its purpose.", "Their teacher advised them to create categories: places to learn, places to rest, places to share, and places where people worked together. The categories helped the map become readable without deciding which places mattered most.", "Mara noticed that several pins were clustered around the market while the eastern streets had almost none. They had not visited those streets yet. The atlas, she realised, showed the limits of their knowledge as clearly as it showed the neighbourhood.")),
                StoryPart(5, "The Street Without a Sign", listOf("On the eastern side, they found a small courtyard where residents grew herbs in reused containers. There was no sign at the entrance, only a painted blue stone beside the gate.", "A young gardener named Kelechi explained that the courtyard had been started by tenants who wanted a shared outdoor space. The landlord had agreed to let them use it as long as they kept the walkway clear.", "Mara asked what the courtyard was called. Kelechi shrugged. The residents had never settled on a name, so Mara wrote 'Blue Stone Courtyard' as a temporary label and left a blank line for the neighbours to choose their own.")),
                StoryPart(6, "The Missing Corner", listOf("When Mara compared her notes, she discovered that every interview mentioned a tiny corner shop that had closed years earlier. People remembered it as a place where lost property was kept until someone came looking.", "The shop's former owner, Mr Bako, now lived with his daughter across town. He welcomed the students and showed them a box of labelled objects: a school badge, a child's drawing, a library card, and a key whose owner had never returned.", "He said the shop had not been special because of what it sold. It was special because people trusted one another there. Mara asked if she could record his memories, and he agreed on the condition that the atlas include the names of the neighbours who helped.")),
                StoryPart(7, "The Argument over the Title", listOf("The class display committee wanted to call the project 'Hidden Attractions of Our District'. Mara objected. The places were not attractions, and the people who cared for them did not maintain them for visitors.", "Dami thought the title was harmless, and their disagreement made the work awkward for a day. Their teacher suggested they ask the interviewees what the atlas should be called.", "The answers varied, but a child from the reading room offered a phrase that stayed with them: 'The places that hold us.' Mara and Dami agreed it described the project better than any official-sounding title.")),
                StoryPart(8, "The Neighbourhood Walk", listOf("They organised a Saturday walk so residents could check the map. Some corrected street names, others added wheelchair-accessible entrances, opening hours, or warnings that a path became muddy after rain.", "At the Blue Stone Courtyard, neighbours chose the name together and asked Mara to write it in large letters. At the clinic bench, the caretaker requested a note explaining that visitors should keep the entrance clear.", "The walk revealed a small community workshop that Mara had missed. Its members repaired school bags and bicycles at no charge when they could. The atlas gained another pin, and Mara gained a new understanding of how much work happened quietly.")),
                StoryPart(9, "The Exhibition", listOf("The school hall filled with maps, photographs, and short recordings. Mara's atlas was not the biggest display, but visitors spent a long time reading the stories beside each pin.", "Mr Bako came with his daughter and recognised the old shop corner on the map. Mrs Eze brought a stack of book recommendations for the reading room. The clinic caretaker stood beside a photograph of the repaired bench.", "A council representative asked for a copy so the neighbourhood office could use the information when planning walking routes. Mara agreed, provided the stories stayed attached to the map and the residents could request corrections.")),
                StoryPart(10, "The Places We Keep", listOf("The final atlas was printed in a small run and placed in the library, clinic waiting room, and market reading room. A digital copy was updated whenever residents sent a correction or suggested a new place.", "Mara and Dami left several blank pages at the end. They wanted future students to add places they had not discovered yet, because a neighbourhood was never finished being understood.", "On the first page, Mara wrote: 'A map does not only show where things are. It can show what people choose to care for.' The blank spaces no longer looked like mistakes. They were invitations to keep listening.")
                )
            )
        )
    )
}
